/* WISEBYTE LESSONS · Safety & Survival: floods, heat, fire, roads, storms and the bush */
WB.L("flood-safety", "safety", "beginner", "Floods: if it's flooded, forget it",
  "Floodwater that looks shallow and calm can sweep a car off the road in seconds. Here is how floods catch people out, and how to stay on the right side of the water.",
  [
    ["The water that fools us", "Floodwater is deceptive: it hides its depth, its speed and what lies underneath.",
      "Picture a country road after a night of heavy rain. Ahead, brown water covers a dip for twenty metres or so. You know this road, and it looks barely ankle deep. This is exactly the moment emergency services worry about. Floodwater is murky, so you cannot see how deep it is, whether the road beneath has washed away, or whether a branch or a fence is hidden under the surface. The current is often far stronger than it looks from a driver's seat. Driving into floodwater is one of the most common ways people die in Australian floods, which is why state emergency services repeat one short message: if it's flooded, forget it. Turning around costs you a few minutes. Pressing on can cost you your life."],
    ["Why cars float", "It takes surprisingly little moving water to lift a car and carry it away.",
      "A car feels heavy and solid, but it is really a sealed box full of air. Once water gets a few tens of centimetres deep, that box starts to become buoyant, and the tyres lose their grip on the road. Add a current pushing sideways and a small car can be lifted and swept off a causeway into a creek. Larger four wheel drives are not immune: they are taller, but they also present a bigger side for the water to push against. Walking is no safer. Fast flowing water only around knee height can knock an adult off their feet, and once you are down, debris and the current make it very hard to stand again. Emergency services often say the same thing: never drive, walk, ride or swim through floodwater."],
    ["Know your flood risk", "Many Australians live in flood prone areas without realising it.",
      "Australia's rivers can rise slowly over days or explode in minutes. Riverine floods build as rain upstream drains into big catchments, giving towns hours or days of warning. Flash floods follow intense downpours, often in hilly or built up areas, where drains overflow and creeks rise almost instantly. Storm surge from cyclones can flood low lying coastal land. A good first step is to find out whether your home, work or regular routes are at risk. Many local councils publish flood maps, and state emergency service websites explain local risks. It also pays to notice the small clues: a street name like Creek Road, a low dip on the way to school, or a causeway with depth markers. Those markers are there for a reason, and it is worth trusting them."],
    ["Warnings and where to find them", "Australia uses a shared warning system, and the Bureau of Meteorology issues flood watches and warnings.",
      "Australia now uses a national Australian Warning System with three levels, each with a colour and a clear action. Advice means an incident has started and you should stay up to date. Watch and Act means conditions are changing and you need to take action now to protect yourself. Emergency Warning is the highest level: you are in danger and must act immediately. Alongside this, the Bureau of Meteorology issues flood watches and flood warnings for river systems. Good places to check include your state emergency service website and app, the Bureau's website, and local ABC radio, which acts as an emergency broadcaster. Do not wait for an official warning before acting if water is rising around you. Warnings help, but your own eyes on a rising creek matter too."],
    ["Getting ready before the rain", "Preparation done in dry weather makes the stressful part far easier.",
      "The best time to prepare for a flood is on a sunny day. Write a simple household plan that covers where you would go if you had to leave, such as friends or family on higher ground, and how you would get there without crossing flooded roads. Put together an emergency kit with a torch, a battery radio, medicines, copies of important documents, chargers and water. Think about pets and livestock early, because moving animals takes time. Around the house, store valuables and chemicals up high and know how to turn off electricity and gas. Check what your insurance actually covers, because flood cover is not automatic in every policy. If you need help with a flood or storm emergency that is not life threatening, the SES can be reached on 132 500."],
    ["When the water is rising", "Leave early, take the safest route and call Triple Zero (000) if life is at risk.",
      "If authorities tell you to evacuate, go, and go early. Roads can be cut long before water reaches your door, and leaving late often means trying to cross rising water in the dark. If you are trapped by floodwater and your life is in danger, call Triple Zero (000). Move to the highest safe point of the building, take a phone, a torch and warm clothes, and wait for rescue rather than trying to swim out. Never let children play in floodwater or stormwater drains, which can suck people in. Floodwater can also carry sewage, chemicals, sharp debris and displaced snakes and spiders. Avoid touching it where you can, and wash well if you do. This lesson is general information only; follow the advice of your state emergency service."],
    ["After the flood", "The danger does not end when the water drops.",
      "When floodwater recedes, it leaves a new set of hazards. Only return home when authorities say it is safe. Power lines may be down, and any electrical appliance or wiring that has been underwater should be checked by a licensed electrician before it is used. Gas leaks are possible, so if you smell gas, leave and call for help. Walls and floors may be weakened, and mud can hide broken glass, nails and animals. Wear gloves, sturdy boots and long sleeves while cleaning, and wash your hands often. Throw out any food that has touched floodwater, and check with your local council or water authority before drinking tap water. Floods are also emotionally exhausting, so check on neighbours and do not be shy about asking for help yourself."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What is the core message state emergency services use about floodwater on roads?", ["Drive slowly through it", "If it's flooded, forget it", "Only cross in a four wheel drive", "Check the depth by walking in first"], 1, "The safest choice is never to enter floodwater at all."],
    [1, "Why can a car be swept away in fairly shallow moving water?", ["Its engine floods and explodes", "Water makes the brakes fail instantly", "It is full of air, so it becomes buoyant and the tyres lose grip", "Only very old cars float"], 2, "A car is a sealed box of air, so even modest depths can lift it and let the current push it."],
    [2, "What usually makes a flash flood different from a riverine flood?", ["It rises very quickly after intense rain", "It only happens in deserts", "It always gives days of warning", "It is caused only by cyclones"], 0, "Flash floods follow intense downpours and can rise in minutes."],
    [3, "In the Australian Warning System, what does an Emergency Warning mean?", ["Stay up to date", "Conditions are changing, prepare to act", "The emergency is over", "You are in danger and must act immediately"], 3, "Emergency Warning is the highest level and calls for immediate action."],
    [5, "If you are trapped by rising floodwater and your life is in danger, what should you do?", ["Swim to the nearest road", "Call Triple Zero (000) and move to the highest safe point", "Wait until morning before calling anyone", "Drive out through the water"], 1, "Call 000 and move to higher ground in the building rather than trying to swim out."]
  ]);

WB.L("heatwave-safety", "safety", "beginner", "Surviving a heatwave",
  "Heatwaves do not burn houses or wash away roads, yet they are one of Australia's deadliest natural hazards. Here is how heat harms the body, and how to stay safe when the mercury soars.",
  [
    ["The quiet killer", "Heatwaves are often described as Australia's deadliest natural hazard, largely because their danger is easy to underestimate.",
      "When a bushfire or cyclone strikes, the danger is obvious: smoke, flames, roaring wind. A heatwave is different. The sky is blue, the streets look normal, and most people simply feel uncomfortable. Yet heatwaves are often described by Australian emergency and health agencies as having caused more deaths than any other natural hazard in the country's recorded history. Much of the harm happens quietly, indoors, to older people living alone or to people with health conditions whose bodies cannot cope with days of relentless heat. Hot nights make it worse, because the body never gets a chance to recover. The Bureau of Meteorology publishes heatwave forecasts that rate events as low intensity, severe or extreme. When a severe or extreme heatwave is forecast, it is time to plan, not just to complain."],
    ["How your body handles heat", "Your body cools itself mainly by sweating, and that system has limits.",
      "Your body works best at a core temperature of around 37 degrees. When it heats up, blood vessels near the skin widen to shed heat, and you sweat. As sweat evaporates, it carries heat away. This is a clever system, but it depends on two things: enough water in your body to make sweat, and air dry enough for sweat to evaporate. In humid conditions, sweat just drips off without cooling you much. If you lose lots of fluid without replacing it, you become dehydrated and the system starts to falter. Your heart works harder, pushing blood to the skin and to working muscles at the same time. For people with heart conditions, or for the very old and very young, that extra strain can be dangerous."],
    ["Who is most at risk", "Anyone can be harmed by heat, but some people are far more vulnerable.",
      "Heat can affect anyone, including fit young people working or exercising hard outdoors. Some groups, though, need special care. Older people, especially those over about 75 or living alone, may not feel thirst as strongly and may not notice they are overheating. Babies and young children heat up quickly and depend on adults to keep them cool and hydrated. Pregnant women, people with heart, kidney or lung conditions, people with diabetes and people taking certain medicines can all be more sensitive to heat. Outdoor workers, people experiencing homelessness and people without air conditioning or with high power bills may struggle to escape the heat at all. During a heatwave, one of the most useful things you can do is check on vulnerable neighbours, friends and relatives, ideally at least once a day."],
    ["Keeping cool", "Stay hydrated, stay out of the hottest part of the day and keep your home as cool as possible.",
      "The basics are simple but they work. Drink water regularly through the day, even before you feel thirsty, and go easy on alcohol and very sugary drinks. If your doctor has told you to limit fluids, ask them what to do in hot weather. Plan errands and exercise for the cooler early morning or evening, and rest during the middle of the day. Wear light, loose clothing and a hat outdoors. At home, close curtains and blinds on sunny windows early, and open windows when it cools down outside. Fans help, but in very high temperatures they may not cool you enough on their own. A cool shower, a damp cloth on the neck, or a few hours in an air conditioned library or shopping centre can all make a real difference."],
    ["Never leave anyone in a car", "A parked car can become dangerously hot within minutes, even with the windows down a little.",
      "On a warm day, the inside of a closed car can quickly become far hotter than the air outside, often by tens of degrees. Much of the rise happens in the first few minutes, and leaving a window slightly open does very little to help. Children are especially vulnerable because their bodies heat up faster than adults' and they cannot get themselves out. Pets are at serious risk too. The rule is simple: never leave children, vulnerable adults or animals in a parked car, not even for a quick dash into a shop. If you see a child or animal locked in a hot car and they appear distressed, call Triple Zero (000) straight away. Make a habit of checking the back seat every time."],
    ["Heat exhaustion and heatstroke", "Heatstroke is a medical emergency: call Triple Zero (000) and start cooling the person.",
      "Heat illness often starts with heat cramps or heat exhaustion. Signs of heat exhaustion include heavy sweating, pale skin, weakness, dizziness, headache, nausea and fainting. Move the person somewhere cool, have them lie down, loosen clothing, cool them with water and fans, and give small sips of water if they are fully alert. If they do not improve quickly, seek medical help. Heatstroke is more serious and can be fatal. The body's cooling has failed. The person may become confused, slur their words, collapse, have a seizure or lose consciousness, and their skin may be hot and either dry or sweaty. Call Triple Zero (000) immediately. While you wait, cool them with water, wet sheets, fans and cold packs in the armpits, groin and neck. Do not give drinks to someone who is not fully conscious."],
    ["Planning for the next one", "A simple heat plan, made in advance, protects you and the people around you.",
      "Heatwaves are becoming more common and more intense in Australia, so it makes sense to plan for them the way you might plan for a storm. Before summer, check that fans and air conditioners work, and think about where you could go to cool off if the power fails. Keep a supply of water and easy food that does not need cooking. Store medicines as the label directs, since some are damaged by heat. Make a list of people you will check on, and ask someone to check on you if you live alone. Watch the Bureau of Meteorology heatwave forecasts and your state health department's heat advice. This lesson is general information, not medical advice: healthdirect (1800 022 222) can help, and doing a first aid course is a great idea."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Why are heatwaves especially dangerous compared with more dramatic disasters?", ["They only affect people in the desert", "Their danger is easy to underestimate, and much harm happens quietly indoors", "They always cause power lines to fall", "They are impossible to forecast"], 1, "Heatwaves look ordinary, so people underestimate them while vulnerable people suffer out of sight."],
    [1, "Why does humid weather make heat harder to cope with?", ["Humidity stops you sweating at all", "Humid air raises your heart rate directly", "Sweat evaporates less, so it cools you less", "Humid air contains less oxygen"], 2, "Cooling depends on sweat evaporating, which happens less in humid air."],
    [4, "What does leaving a car window slightly open do on a hot day?", ["Keeps the car safely cool", "Very little to stop the temperature rising", "Makes the car cooler than outside", "Is enough for pets but not children"], 1, "A slightly open window barely slows the dangerous rise in temperature inside a parked car."],
    [5, "Someone in the heat becomes confused and collapses. What is the right first action?", ["Give them a large drink of water", "Let them sleep it off", "Drive them home", "Call Triple Zero (000) and start cooling them"], 3, "These are signs of heatstroke, a medical emergency needing 000 and rapid cooling."],
    [3, "When is the best time to exercise or run errands during a heatwave?", ["Early morning or evening", "Around midday", "Mid afternoon", "Whenever it is sunniest"], 0, "The cooler parts of the day put less strain on your body."]
  ]);

WB.L("home-fire-safety", "safety", "beginner", "Home fires: alarms, escape plans and kitchen fires",
  "A house fire can make a home deadly in a matter of minutes. A working smoke alarm and a practised escape plan are the two things most likely to get everyone out alive.",
  [
    ["Minutes, not hours", "A house fire can become unsurvivable very quickly, so early warning and a fast exit matter most.",
      "In films, people stroll through burning houses calmly. Real house fires are nothing like that. Modern homes are full of synthetic materials such as foam couches, plastics and carpets that burn hot and fast and release thick, toxic smoke. Fire services warn that a room can become deadly within a few minutes. The smoke, not the flames, is usually what kills, because it fills the air with poisonous gases like carbon monoxide and makes it impossible to see. Many fatal house fires happen at night, when people are asleep. Smoke does not wake you up; it can make you sleep more deeply. That is why fire services focus on two simple things: hearing the warning early, and getting out fast without stopping to collect belongings."],
    ["Smoke alarms that work", "Working smoke alarms are required by law in Australian homes, and they need regular care.",
      "Every Australian state and territory requires smoke alarms in homes, but the exact rules differ. Queensland, for example, has been moving to interconnected photoelectric alarms in every bedroom, hallway and level of a home, so when one sounds they all sound. Fire services generally recommend photoelectric alarms, because they are good at detecting the smouldering fires that often start at night. An alarm only helps if it works. A common guideline is to test each alarm monthly by pressing the test button, to clean dust from the vents regularly, and to replace alarms that are about ten years old. If your alarm uses a replaceable battery, change it as the manufacturer recommends. Check your state fire service website for the current rules where you live, especially if you rent or are selling."],
    ["Your escape plan", "Know two ways out of every room and agree on a meeting place outside.",
      "When an alarm sounds at 2am, you will not have time to think things through. That is why fire services encourage every household to draw a simple floor plan and mark two ways out of each room, usually a door and a window. Make sure security screens and deadlocks can be opened quickly from inside, and keep keys in the lock or nearby when you are home. Choose a safe meeting place outside, such as the letterbox or a neighbour's driveway, so you can count heads. Practise the plan with everyone in the home, including children, at least once or twice a year. Children sometimes hide from fire under beds or in cupboards, so teach them that the right thing to do is get out and stay out."],
    ["Getting out alive", "Get down low, close doors behind you and never go back inside.",
      "If there is smoke, get down low, where the air is cleaner and cooler, and crawl to the nearest safe exit. Before opening a closed door, feel it with the back of your hand. If it is hot, use your other way out. Close doors behind you as you leave, because a closed door can slow the spread of fire and smoke. Once you are out, call Triple Zero (000) and stay out. Never go back inside for pets, phones or valuables, as many people have died doing exactly that; tell firefighters if anyone is missing. If you cannot get out, close the door, block gaps with towels or clothing, open a window if safe, and call 000. If your clothes catch fire, stop, drop to the ground, cover your face and roll."],
    ["Kitchen fires", "Keep looking while cooking, and never put water on burning oil.",
      "The kitchen is where many house fires start, often when someone leaves food cooking and gets distracted. Fire services sum it up neatly: keep looking while cooking. If a pan of oil or fat catches fire, never throw water on it. Water sinks under the hot oil, flashes into steam and can blast burning oil across the kitchen in a fireball. Instead, if it is safe, turn off the heat and smother the flames by sliding a lid or a fire blanket over the pan, then leave it covered until it has completely cooled. Do not try to carry the burning pan outside. A fire blanket mounted near the kitchen exit is cheap and easy to use. If the fire is spreading or you are unsure, get everyone out, close the door and call Triple Zero (000)."],
    ["Heaters, power and batteries", "Many fires start from heaters, overloaded power points and damaged lithium batteries.",
      "Winter brings a rise in house fires as heaters, electric blankets and clothes dryers work harder. Keep heaters at least a metre away from curtains, bedding, clothes and furniture, and turn them off when you leave the room or go to bed. Check electric blankets for frayed cords or worn spots before winter. Clean the lint filter of your clothes dryer every time you use it. Avoid overloading power boards, and never cover them with rugs. A newer worry is lithium ion batteries in e-bikes, e-scooters and power tools. Damaged or poor quality batteries can overheat and burn fiercely. Use the charger that came with the device, avoid charging bulky batteries overnight or near your exit path, and stop using any battery that is swollen, hot or damaged."],
    ["Burns first aid", "Cool a burn under running water for 20 minutes, and call Triple Zero (000) for serious burns.",
      "If someone is burned, the first aid advice in Australia is clear: cool the burn under cool running water for 20 minutes. This helps even if a little time has passed. Remove jewellery and clothing from the area unless it is stuck to the skin. Do not use ice, butter, toothpaste or creams. After cooling, cover the burn loosely with plastic cling wrap or a clean, non stick dressing, and keep the person warm. Call Triple Zero (000) for burns that are large, deep, on the face, hands, feet, groin or airway, caused by chemicals or electricity, or on a child or older person. This lesson is general information only. A proper first aid course teaches these skills hands on, and it is one of the best investments a household can make."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What usually kills people in house fires?", ["Falling roofs", "Smoke and toxic gases", "Electrical shocks", "Water from fire hoses"], 1, "Toxic smoke, not flames, is the main killer in house fires."],
    [1, "What type of smoke alarm do fire services generally recommend?", ["Photoelectric", "Heat only", "Carbon dioxide", "Motion sensing"], 0, "Photoelectric alarms are good at detecting smouldering fires that often start at night."],
    [3, "Why should you close doors behind you as you escape a fire?", ["To keep out burglars", "It is required by law", "To slow the spread of fire and smoke", "So firefighters know the room is empty"], 2, "A closed door can hold back fire and smoke, buying precious time."],
    [4, "A pan of oil catches fire on the stove. What should you NOT do?", ["Turn off the heat if safe", "Slide a lid over the pan", "Use a fire blanket", "Throw water on it"], 3, "Water on burning oil can cause a violent fireball."],
    [6, "How long should a burn be cooled under running water?", ["About 20 minutes", "About 1 minute", "Until it stops hurting, then apply butter", "About 5 seconds"], 0, "Australian first aid advice is 20 minutes of cool running water."]
  ]);

WB.L("emergency-kit-plan", "safety", "beginner", "Get ready: your emergency kit and household plan",
  "When disaster strikes, the decisions you make in the first hour matter most. A little planning on a quiet weekend can make those decisions almost automatic.",
  [
    ["Why plan at all", "People who plan ahead cope better in emergencies and recover faster afterwards.",
      "Imagine your phone buzzes at midnight with an emergency warning. Water is rising, or a fire is moving toward town, and you have perhaps half an hour. Where are the car keys? Who is picking up the kids from their other parent's house? What about the dog, the medicines and the passports? In the middle of a crisis, stress makes clear thinking hard, and people waste time or make risky choices. Emergency organisations such as the Australian Red Cross and state emergency services encourage every household to plan ahead for exactly this reason. Being prepared does not mean expecting the worst every day. It means doing the thinking once, in calm conditions, so that in a real emergency you can act quickly and spend your energy on staying safe."],
    ["Know your risks", "Different places face different hazards, so start by learning which ones apply to you.",
      "Australia's hazards vary enormously by region. Northern coasts face cyclones and storm surge. River towns face floods. Bushland edges face fire. Cities face heatwaves, severe storms and the occasional power or water outage. Almost everyone could face a house fire. Start your plan by finding out which hazards are most likely where you live, work and travel. Your local council and state emergency service websites usually explain local risks, and many publish flood or bushfire maps. Talk to neighbours who have lived in the area for years, because they often remember events that newer residents have never seen. Once you know your risks, your plan can focus on the things that matter most for you, rather than trying to prepare for every possible disaster at once."],
    ["Making a household plan", "A good plan answers where you will go, how you will get there and how you will stay in touch.",
      "A household emergency plan does not need to be complicated. It should answer a few key questions. When will you leave, and what will trigger that decision? Where will you go: a friend or relative's place in a safer area, or an evacuation centre? How will you get there, and what is your back up route if roads are closed? How will family members contact each other if phones are down or you are split up? Many families choose one contact person who lives in another area. Write down important numbers on paper too, in case your phone battery dies. The Australian Red Cross offers free planning tools, including its Get Prepared app. Share the plan with everyone in the household and review it each year."],
    ["What goes in the kit", "An emergency kit holds the basics to get through the first few days without help.",
      "An emergency kit is a collection of supplies stored together, ready to grab or to use at home. Common recommendations include drinking water, with a common guideline of around three litres per person per day for at least three days, and food that does not need cooking or refrigeration. Add a torch, a battery powered radio and spare batteries, a first aid kit, a power bank and charging cables, and some cash in case card machines are down. Include medicines and prescriptions, glasses, toiletries, and anything needed for babies, older family members or people with a disability. Keep copies of important documents, such as identification and insurance papers, in a waterproof bag or saved securely online. Check the kit every six months or so and replace anything out of date."],
    ["Pets and animals", "Animals are part of the family, so include them in your plan from the start.",
      "People have sometimes delayed evacuating, or gone back into danger, because they could not take their pets. Planning ahead can prevent this. Make sure pets are microchipped and that your contact details are up to date, and keep a recent photo of each animal on your phone. Pack food, water, leads, medicines and a carrier or crate in your kit. Find out in advance which friends, boarding kennels or evacuation centres can take animals, since not every centre can. For horses and livestock, plans take much longer to carry out, so owners often need to move animals early, well before any official warning. Some agriculture departments and animal welfare groups provide guides for large animals. The key idea is simple: decide early, so you never have to choose between your animals and your safety."],
    ["Staying informed", "Know where official warnings come from, and have a way to receive them if the power goes out.",
      "During an emergency, reliable information can be the difference between leaving in time and being trapped. Australia uses a national warning system with three levels: Advice, Watch and Act, and Emergency Warning. Each state and territory has its own emergency websites and apps, so find the ones for your area before you need them. The Bureau of Meteorology provides weather and flood warnings, and ABC local radio is an official emergency broadcaster that often keeps broadcasting when other services fail. A battery or wind up radio is useful if power and mobile networks go down. Be careful with social media. It can spread useful local news quickly, but rumours and old photos also circulate. Check official sources before acting, and if your life is in danger, call Triple Zero (000)."],
    ["Looking after each other", "Neighbours are often the first helpers in a disaster, so get to know them now.",
      "In many Australian disasters, the first people to help were not police or firefighters but neighbours. They checked on older residents, shared generators, moved livestock and offered spare rooms. Strong local connections are one of the best predictors of how well a community copes and recovers. You can build them in simple ways: swap phone numbers with neighbours, find out who might need help to evacuate, and join a local community group or volunteer organisation. If you have a medical condition or disability, consider letting trusted neighbours know what support you may need. After a disaster, recovery can take months or years, and stress and grief are normal. Organisations such as the Australian Red Cross, local councils and recovery centres can help. This lesson is general information, so follow official advice for your area."]
  ],
  [
    [2, "Why is it useful to choose one contact person who lives in another area?", ["They can drive you to safety", "Family members can all check in with one person if they are separated", "They will pay for damages", "It is a legal requirement"], 1, "A single out of area contact helps a scattered family stay connected."],
    [3, "What is a common guideline for emergency drinking water?", ["One glass per person per day", "Around three litres per person per day for at least three days", "Ten litres per household in total", "No water is needed if you have food"], 1, "A common guideline is around three litres per person per day for at least three days."],
    [5, "Why is a battery or wind up radio useful in an emergency?", ["It can call Triple Zero", "It charges your car", "It still works if power and mobile networks fail", "It detects floods automatically"], 2, "Radio, including ABC local radio, can keep you informed when other services are down."],
    [4, "Why do owners of horses and livestock often need to act early?", ["Moving large animals takes a long time", "Livestock can sense disasters", "Animals are not allowed in cars", "Insurers require it"], 0, "Moving large animals is slow, so plans need to start well before warnings escalate."],
    [6, "Who are often the first helpers in a disaster?", ["International aid groups", "Insurance assessors", "Federal politicians", "Neighbours"], 3, "Neighbours are frequently first on the scene, which is why local connections matter."]
  ]);

WB.L("storms-cyclones", "safety", "intermediate", "Severe storms and cyclones",
  "Every year, wild storms and tropical cyclones tear roofs off, drop trees on cars and cut power to thousands of homes. Knowing what to do before and during one can keep your family safe.",
  [
    ["Storm country", "Severe storms can strike almost anywhere in Australia, and cyclones threaten the north.",
      "On a hot spring afternoon in south east Queensland or western Sydney, towering clouds can build into a storm that drops giant hail, flattens fences and floods streets in less than an hour. Severe thunderstorms happen across most of Australia, bringing damaging winds, large hail, heavy rain, flash flooding and lightning. In the tropical north, the bigger threat is the tropical cyclone, a vast spinning storm system that forms over warm ocean water. The official cyclone season in Australia runs from November to April, and cyclones affect coasts from Western Australia across the Northern Territory to Queensland, and sometimes travel further south. The Bureau of Meteorology issues severe thunderstorm and cyclone warnings, and state emergency services, reachable on 132 500, help with storm damage in non life threatening situations."],
    ["How cyclones are rated", "Australia rates cyclones from Category 1 to Category 5 based on their strongest wind gusts.",
      "The Bureau of Meteorology uses a five step scale for tropical cyclones. A Category 1 cyclone has damaging gales that can damage trees, caravans and crops. As the categories climb, wind gusts become far stronger, and Category 5 cyclones can bring extremely destructive winds capable of causing widespread devastation. Category 3 and above are described as severe tropical cyclones. However, the category only describes wind. A weaker cyclone can still be deadly through flooding rain, which can spread hundreds of kilometres inland, or through storm surge. Cyclones are also unpredictable, sometimes strengthening rapidly or changing direction. So it is wise not to relax because a cyclone is 'only' a low category. Follow the Bureau's track maps and warnings closely, and act on advice from your local emergency services."],
    ["The danger of storm surge", "Storm surge can push the sea far inland and is one of a cyclone's deadliest effects.",
      "When a strong cyclone approaches a coast, its powerful winds push ocean water toward the shore, and the low air pressure at its centre lets the sea bulge upward. The result is a storm surge: a rise in sea level well above the normal high tide, topped with large waves. If it arrives at high tide, the effect can be devastating, flooding low lying coastal suburbs, roads and caravan parks. Around the world, storm surge has caused many of the deadliest cyclone disasters in history. In Australia, authorities may order people in storm surge zones to evacuate even if their homes are well built, because the threat is from water rather than wind. If you live near the coast in cyclone country, find out whether you are in a storm surge zone."],
    ["Preparing your home", "Simple jobs before storm season can stop much of the damage.",
      "Many storm injuries and much property damage come from flying debris and falling trees. Before the season, clean gutters and downpipes so heavy rain can drain away. Trim overhanging branches, ideally using a qualified arborist for large trees. Check that your roof is in good repair. Make a list of loose outdoor items, such as trampolines, outdoor furniture, bins and pot plants, that you would secure or bring inside when a storm or cyclone is forecast. In cyclone areas, residents often prepare by fitting shutters or protecting windows, and by knowing whether their home was built to modern cyclone standards. Keep your emergency kit stocked, fill vehicles with fuel, and charge phones and power banks when a warning is issued. Park cars under cover or away from trees if hail is forecast."],
    ["Sheltering safely", "Shelter in the strongest part of the building, away from windows, and stay inside until it is truly over.",
      "When a severe storm or cyclone hits, the safest place is usually indoors in the strongest part of the building. This is often a small internal room such as a bathroom, laundry or hallway, away from windows and glass doors. If the building starts to break up, protect yourself under a sturdy table or mattress. Unplug electrical appliances and avoid using landline phones during lightning. In a cyclone, be very careful of the calm eye. When the centre passes over, the wind can drop suddenly and the sky may even clear, tempting people outside. Then the winds return, often from the opposite direction and just as strongly. Stay inside until authorities say the cyclone has passed. If you are in danger at any time, call Triple Zero (000)."],
    ["Lightning", "If you can hear thunder, you are close enough to be struck by lightning.",
      "Lightning can strike many kilometres away from the heaviest rain, which is why safety agencies say that if you can hear thunder, you are within range. A good habit is to head indoors or into a hard topped car as soon as you hear it, and to wait a good while after the last rumble before going back outside. Avoid open fields, beaches, water, hilltops, isolated trees and metal fences. Swimmers, golfers, campers and people working outdoors are particularly exposed. Indoors, stay off corded phones and away from plumbing and electrical appliances during the storm. If someone is struck by lightning, it is safe to touch them, because people do not hold an electrical charge. Call Triple Zero (000) straight away, and begin CPR if they are not breathing normally."],
    ["After the storm", "Fallen power lines, damaged buildings and floodwater remain serious hazards.",
      "Once the wind dies down, the clean up brings its own risks. Always assume fallen power lines are live. Stay well clear of them, at least 8 metres according to many electricity networks, and remember that water, fences and wet ground near a fallen line can be electrified too. Report them to your electricity distributor, or call Triple Zero (000) if there is immediate danger. Do not enter badly damaged buildings, and watch out for broken glass, exposed nails and asbestos in older homes. Avoid driving through floodwater or under damaged trees. Chainsaw and ladder injuries are common after storms, so be cautious or leave big jobs to professionals and the SES. Photograph damage before clearing it for insurance. This lesson is general information; always follow official advice for your area."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "When does Australia's official tropical cyclone season run?", ["November to April", "June to September", "All year equally", "January to March only"], 0, "The official season runs from November to April."],
    [1, "Why should you not relax just because a cyclone is a low category?", ["Low categories always strengthen to Category 5", "Category only measures wind, and rain or storm surge can still be deadly", "Low categories are only rated at night", "Category 1 cyclones move faster"], 1, "The category describes wind, but flooding rain and storm surge can still kill."],
    [2, "Why might people in a storm surge zone be told to evacuate even if their homes are strong?", ["Insurance rules", "Power will be cut", "Strong homes attract lightning", "The threat comes from rising sea water rather than wind"], 3, "Storm surge floods low lying land, so even sturdy homes can be dangerous."],
    [4, "What is dangerous about the eye of a cyclone?", ["It brings the strongest rain", "It causes earthquakes", "The calm can tempt people outside before winds return", "It always brings hail"], 2, "Winds return after the eye passes, often from the opposite direction."],
    [6, "How should you treat a fallen power line?", ["Move it with a dry stick", "Assume it is live and stay well clear", "Touch it briefly to check", "Drive over it slowly"], 1, "Always assume fallen lines are live and keep well away."]
  ]);

WB.L("driving-long-distance", "safety", "intermediate", "Staying alive on long drives",
  "Australia's long, empty roads are beautiful, but fatigue, wildlife and remote breakdowns catch out even experienced drivers. Here is how to make every road trip end safely.",
  [
    ["The long road", "Country roads carry a disproportionate share of Australia's road deaths.",
      "There is something hypnotic about a long Australian highway: straight bitumen, red dirt, a horizon that never seems to get closer. That hypnotic quality is part of the danger. Although most Australians live in cities, a large share of road deaths happen on rural and regional roads, often in single vehicle crashes where a car leaves the road or hits a tree. Speeds are higher, roads may be narrow or unsealed, help can be hours away, and the scenery can lull drivers into lapses of attention. Road safety campaigns often talk about the 'fatal five': speeding, drink and drug driving, not wearing a seatbelt, fatigue and distraction. On long drives, fatigue and distraction become especially important, because they creep up on people who feel perfectly fine when they set out."],
    ["Fatigue and microsleeps", "Tired driving can be as dangerous as drink driving, and you cannot will yourself to stay awake.",
      "Fatigue is one of the biggest killers on Australian roads. Being awake for a long time impairs your reactions and judgement in a way research has compared to having alcohol in your blood. The most frightening part is the microsleep: a few seconds when your brain simply switches off, often without you realising. At 100 kilometres per hour, a car travels close to 28 metres every second, so a four second microsleep can carry you well over 100 metres with nobody in control. Warning signs include yawning, sore or heavy eyes, drifting in your lane, missing signs and not remembering the last few kilometres. Opening the window or turning up the music does not fix tiredness. The only real cure is sleep, so pull over somewhere safe and have a rest or a power nap."],
    ["Planning to beat fatigue", "Get a good night's sleep, plan breaks every two hours and share the driving.",
      "Fatigue is much easier to prevent than to fight. Start a long drive well rested, and avoid setting out after a full day of work or in the hours when your body normally sleeps, such as late at night and in the mid afternoon slump. Road safety agencies commonly recommend a break of at least 15 minutes every two hours. Get out, walk around and have a drink and a snack. Look out for Driver Reviver sites, which often operate on major highways during holiday periods and offer free tea, coffee and a friendly chat. If you can, share the driving with another licensed driver. Be honest about how far you can go. An extra night in a motel is a small price compared with the cost of a crash."],
    ["Wildlife on the road", "Brake firmly in a straight line rather than swerving hard to avoid an animal.",
      "Kangaroos, wallabies, wombats, emus and livestock are a real hazard on country roads, especially around dawn and dusk, when many native animals are most active, and at night. Slow down in areas with wildlife warning signs and scan the roadside verges, where animal eyes may reflect your headlights. If an animal jumps out in front of you, the general advice is to brake firmly in a straight line and avoid swerving violently. Many serious crashes happen when drivers swerve, lose control and hit a tree or oncoming vehicle, when hitting the animal itself might have caused only damage to the car. Kangaroos are notoriously unpredictable and can change direction suddenly, and where there is one there are often more. If you hit an animal, only stop when it is safe to do so."],
    ["Road trains and unsealed roads", "Give big vehicles plenty of room, and slow right down on gravel.",
      "In outback Australia you may meet road trains, trucks pulling two, three or even more trailers, which can stretch more than 50 metres long. Overtaking one takes a long stretch of clear road and plenty of patience, so only pass when you can see well ahead and be sure it is safe. When a road train is coming the other way on a narrow road, slow down and move left, as it may not be able to move over. Unsealed roads bring their own dangers: loose gravel, corrugations, bulldust holes and sharp edges can all make a car slide. Drive slower than you would on bitumen, avoid harsh braking and steering, and keep your headlights on to be visible in dust. Check road conditions before you go, as roads may close after rain."],
    ["If you break down in remote areas", "In remote areas, stay with your vehicle: it is much easier to find than a person.",
      "Before heading into remote country, tell someone your route and when you expect to arrive, and check in when you do. Carry far more water than you think you will need, along with food, a first aid kit, a spare tyre and tools. Mobile coverage disappears across large parts of Australia, so many remote travellers carry a satellite communication device or a personal locator beacon. If you break down or get stuck, the standard advice is to stay with your vehicle. A car is far easier for searchers to spot than a person walking, and it provides shade and shelter. People have died walking away from a vehicle in search of help, often in extreme heat. Rest in the shade during the hottest part of the day and conserve your water and energy."],
    ["Calling for help", "Know how to call Triple Zero (000) and share your exact location.",
      "If there is a crash, first make sure you are safe from other traffic: switch on hazard lights and, if you can, get yourself and passengers away from the road. Call Triple Zero (000) for any crash where someone is injured or trapped, a vehicle is on fire, or the road is dangerously blocked. On a mobile phone, emergency calls can usually connect through any available network, even if it is not your provider's. The free Emergency+ app, developed by Australian emergency services, can show your GPS location so you can read it to the operator, which is vital on long, featureless roads. Do not move injured people unless they are in immediate danger. This lesson is general information, and a first aid course will prepare you to help at a crash."]
  ],
  [
    [1, "What is a microsleep?", ["A planned power nap", "A few seconds when the brain switches off without warning", "A long sleep at a rest stop", "A sleep disorder only in truck drivers"], 1, "A microsleep is a brief, often unnoticed lapse into sleep, deadly at highway speed."],
    [2, "How often do road safety agencies commonly recommend a break on long drives?", ["Every two hours", "Every six hours", "Only when you feel sleepy", "Once per day"], 0, "A break of at least 15 minutes every two hours is commonly recommended."],
    [3, "A kangaroo jumps in front of your car. What is the general advice?", ["Swerve hard to miss it", "Speed up to pass it", "Brake firmly in a straight line", "Turn off the headlights"], 2, "Swerving violently often causes worse crashes than hitting the animal."],
    [5, "If you break down in a remote area, what is the standard advice?", ["Walk to the nearest town", "Walk only at midday", "Split up to search for help", "Stay with your vehicle"], 3, "A vehicle is easier to spot and provides shelter."],
    [6, "What can the Emergency+ app help you do?", ["Share your GPS location with the 000 operator", "Book a tow truck", "Unlock your car", "Find cheaper fuel"], 0, "Emergency+ shows your location so you can tell the operator exactly where you are."]
  ]);

WB.L("heart-attack-stroke-signs", "safety", "intermediate", "Heart attack and stroke: spotting the signs",
  "Heart attacks and strokes are medical emergencies where every minute counts. Recognising the warning signs, and calling for help fast, can save a life or prevent lifelong disability.",
  [
    ["Time is muscle, time is brain", "In heart attacks and strokes, delay causes damage, so acting fast is everything.",
      "A man in his fifties feels a heavy pressure in his chest while mowing the lawn. He tells himself it is indigestion and sits down for a while. A woman notices her husband's speech has become slurred at dinner, but he says he is just tired. Scenes like these happen in Australian homes every day, and the delay can be devastating. In a heart attack, part of the heart muscle is starved of blood. In most strokes, part of the brain is. The longer the blockage lasts, the more tissue dies. Doctors say 'time is muscle' for the heart and 'time is brain' for strokes. Modern hospital treatments can open blocked vessels, but many work best when given early, which only happens if someone calls for help quickly."],
    ["What happens in a heart attack", "A heart attack happens when a blocked artery stops blood reaching part of the heart.",
      "Your heart muscle is fed by its own blood vessels, the coronary arteries. Over years, fatty plaque can build up inside them. If a plaque cracks, a blood clot can form on top and block the artery. The heart muscle beyond the blockage is then starved of oxygen and begins to die. This is a heart attack, which doctors call a myocardial infarction. It is different from a cardiac arrest, though the two are often confused. In a cardiac arrest, the heart's electrical rhythm goes haywire and it stops pumping effectively, so the person collapses, stops breathing normally and becomes unresponsive. A heart attack can trigger a cardiac arrest, which is one reason a heart attack must never be ignored or treated as something to wait out."],
    ["Heart attack warning signs", "Chest discomfort is common, but symptoms vary and are not always dramatic.",
      "Many people imagine a heart attack as a sudden, crushing collapse. In reality, the signs can be subtle and can come on slowly. The Heart Foundation describes warning signs such as pain, pressure, heaviness or tightness in the chest, which may spread to the arm, shoulder, neck, jaw or back. Other signs include shortness of breath, nausea, a cold sweat, dizziness or feeling light headed. Some people, including many women, older people and people with diabetes, may have less typical symptoms, such as unusual tiredness, breathlessness or discomfort in the back or jaw rather than obvious chest pain. Symptoms can come and go. Because the signs vary so much, it is worth taking any unusual chest discomfort seriously rather than waiting to see whether it passes on its own."],
    ["What to do for a suspected heart attack", "If symptoms are severe, getting worse or lasting more than 10 minutes, call Triple Zero (000).",
      "The Heart Foundation's warning signs action plan is straightforward. If you have warning signs, stop what you are doing and rest. If symptoms are severe, get worse quickly or last longer than 10 minutes, call Triple Zero (000) and ask for an ambulance. Do not drive yourself to hospital, and do not let someone else drive you unless there is truly no alternative, because paramedics can begin treatment straight away and handle a cardiac arrest if it happens. The operator may give advice about medicines such as aspirin, so follow their instructions. If the person collapses and is not breathing normally, start CPR and ask someone to bring a defibrillator. Many public places now have one, and they are designed to be used by untrained bystanders by following the spoken instructions."],
    ["What happens in a stroke", "A stroke is a sudden interruption of blood supply to part of the brain.",
      "There are two main types of stroke. Most are ischaemic strokes, caused when a blood clot or plaque blocks an artery supplying the brain. Others are haemorrhagic strokes, where a blood vessel in or around the brain bursts and bleeds. Either way, brain cells in the affected area are cut off from oxygen and begin to die. Researchers estimate that in a typical large stroke, the brain can lose close to two million nerve cells for every minute it goes untreated. Which abilities are affected depends on which part of the brain is damaged: movement, speech, vision, balance or understanding. Hospitals can now treat many ischaemic strokes with clot dissolving drugs or by physically removing the clot, but these treatments are time sensitive, so getting to hospital fast is crucial."],
    ["Think F.A.S.T.", "Check the Face, Arms and Speech, and if any are affected, it is Time to call Triple Zero (000).",
      "The Stroke Foundation's F.A.S.T. test is a simple way to recognise the most common signs of stroke. Face: check their face. Has their mouth drooped? Arms: can they lift both arms? Speech: is their speech slurred, and do they understand you? Time is critical: if you see any of these signs, call Triple Zero (000) straight away. Other possible signs of stroke include sudden numbness or weakness, especially on one side of the body, sudden loss of vision, dizziness or loss of balance, a sudden severe headache, or trouble swallowing. Note the time the symptoms started, because doctors will ask, and it helps them choose treatment. Do not give the person anything to eat or drink, and stay with them, reassuring them, until the ambulance arrives."],
    ["Mini strokes and prevention", "Symptoms that vanish quickly still need urgent attention, and many risks can be reduced.",
      "Sometimes stroke symptoms appear and then disappear within minutes or hours. This is often a transient ischaemic attack, or TIA, sometimes called a mini stroke. It can be a warning that a major stroke may follow, so it still needs urgent medical attention, even if the person feels fine. For both heart attack and stroke, many risk factors can be reduced: high blood pressure, high cholesterol, smoking, diabetes, physical inactivity, excess weight and heavy drinking. Regular health checks with a GP help find problems early. This lesson is general information, not medical advice. For more, see the Heart Foundation, the Stroke Foundation and healthdirect. In an emergency, always call Triple Zero (000), and consider doing a first aid course, which teaches CPR and defibrillator use hands on."]
  ],
  [
    [1, "What is the difference between a heart attack and a cardiac arrest?", ["They are exactly the same", "A heart attack is a blocked artery; a cardiac arrest is when the heart stops pumping effectively", "A cardiac arrest only affects children", "A heart attack always happens in the brain"], 1, "A heart attack is a blockage, while cardiac arrest is a rhythm failure where the heart stops pumping properly."],
    [2, "Which of these can be a warning sign of a heart attack?", ["Pain spreading to the jaw or back", "An itchy rash on the legs", "A runny nose", "Improved appetite"], 0, "Chest discomfort can spread to the arm, shoulder, neck, jaw or back."],
    [3, "Why should a person having a heart attack not drive themselves to hospital?", ["Hospitals refuse walk in patients", "Paramedics can start treatment and manage a cardiac arrest", "It is illegal to drive after dark", "Ambulances are always faster than cars"], 1, "Paramedics can treat immediately and handle a collapse on the way."],
    [5, "In the F.A.S.T. test, what does the A stand for?", ["Airway", "Alertness", "Arms", "Ambulance"], 2, "A is for Arms: can the person lift both arms?"],
    [6, "Stroke symptoms disappear after 15 minutes. What should happen?", ["Nothing, it has passed", "Wait a week and see", "Take a nap", "Seek urgent medical attention, as it may be a TIA"], 3, "A TIA can warn of a coming major stroke, so it still needs urgent care."]
  ]);

WB.L("lost-in-the-bush", "safety", "advanced", "Lost in the bush: how to be found",
  "Every year in Australia, bushwalkers, campers and tourists get lost, and most are found alive. The ones who do best usually prepared before they left and stayed calm once things went wrong.",
  [
    ["How people get lost", "Getting lost usually starts with small, ordinary mistakes rather than a dramatic event.",
      "Few people set out to have an adventure that ends in a police search. More often, it begins with something small. A walker follows a faint side trail that looks like a shortcut. A day hike runs late and the light fades. Mist rolls in on a ridge, and every gum tree suddenly looks the same. A tired walker takes a wrong turn at an unmarked junction and keeps going, hoping the track will reconnect. Search and rescue teams know these patterns well. People rarely realise they are lost straight away, and the urge to keep moving, to fix the problem quickly, often carries them further from where searchers will look. Understanding how it happens is the first step to avoiding it, and to responding well if it happens to you."],
    ["Before you go", "Leave a trip plan, check conditions and carry the right gear, including a way to call for help.",
      "The single most important safety step happens before you leave home. Tell a reliable person where you are going, your route, who is with you, and when you expect to be back, along with a time when they should raise the alarm. Some national parks also have walker registration or trip intention forms. Check the weather forecast, fire danger ratings and park alerts. Carry more water than you expect to need, extra food, warm and waterproof layers, a torch, a first aid kit, and a map with a compass or a GPS device, and know how to use them. Do not rely on a phone alone, since coverage is patchy and batteries die. Many experienced walkers carry a personal locator beacon, and in some areas you can borrow one free."],
    ["Personal locator beacons", "A PLB can send a distress signal with your location to rescuers via satellite, even with no phone coverage.",
      "A personal locator beacon, or PLB, is a small device that, when activated, sends a distress signal via satellite to search and rescue authorities. In Australia, these signals are received by the Australian Maritime Safety Authority, which coordinates with police and other agencies. Most modern beacons include GPS, so rescuers receive your location accurately, often within minutes. PLBs work almost anywhere outdoors with a clear view of the sky, far beyond mobile coverage, and do not need a subscription. You should register your beacon with AMSA, which is free, so rescuers know who you are and who to contact. Only activate it in a genuine life threatening emergency. Some newer smartphones and satellite messengers offer emergency satellite messaging too, but check exactly how your device works before relying on it."],
    ["Stop and think", "If you realise you are lost, stop moving, stay calm and make a plan.",
      "The moment you realise you do not know where you are, the most useful thing you can do is stop. Many outdoor organisations teach the idea of STOP: Stop, Think, Observe, Plan. Sit down, have a drink of water and let the first rush of panic pass. Think about when you last knew for certain where you were. Observe your surroundings, the time of day, the weather and how much water, food and battery you have. Then make a plan. If you can confidently retrace your steps to a known point, that may be sensible. If not, the best plan is usually to stay where you are, especially if someone knows your trip plan. Wandering further makes you harder to find, burns energy and water, and can lead into steep or dangerous country."],
    ["Shelter, water and warmth", "Protecting yourself from the weather is often more urgent than finding food.",
      "Survival instructors often use a rough rule of threes as a reminder of priorities: a person can survive around three hours in harsh conditions without shelter, around three days without water and around three weeks without food. The numbers vary greatly, but the order is useful. In hot conditions, find shade, rest during the heat of the day and ration your effort rather than your water, drinking what you need. In cold, wet or windy conditions, which occur in alpine areas and Tasmania even in summer, hypothermia is a serious danger. Put on all your layers, get out of the wind, and insulate yourself from the cold ground with leaves, a pack or anything dry. Food matters least. Most people can go several days without eating and still be found in reasonable health."],
    ["Helping searchers find you", "Make yourself big, bright and noisy, and stay close to open ground.",
      "Searchers on foot, in vehicles and in helicopters will be looking for you, so make yourself easy to spot. Choose a spot near a clearing, track or open ridge if you can reach one safely, as people under thick canopy are very hard to see from the air. Lay out bright clothing, a space blanket or other colourful gear. Three of anything, such as three whistle blasts, three flashes of light or three piles of rocks, is widely recognised as a distress signal. A whistle carries much further than a voice and uses far less energy. A small mirror, or even a phone screen, can flash sunlight toward aircraft. If you have a phone, turn it off or into low power mode between checks, so it lasts long enough to be useful."],
    ["Calling for help", "If you have any signal, call Triple Zero (000), and keep your phone charged for searchers.",
      "If you have mobile reception, call Triple Zero (000) and ask for police, who coordinate land searches in Australia. In many cases, an emergency call from a mobile can connect through another carrier's network if yours has no coverage. The Emergency+ app can show your GPS coordinates to read to the operator. Once you have reported your situation, stay where you are unless told otherwise, and follow instructions carefully. If you cannot get a call through, a text message to family may sometimes slip through when a call will not. Remember too that being found is not the end of the story: rescuers may need to treat dehydration, hypothermia or injuries. This lesson is general information, not a substitute for proper training, so consider a first aid and outdoor navigation course."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What often makes lost walkers harder to find?", ["Staying in one spot", "The urge to keep moving to fix the problem quickly", "Wearing bright clothing", "Carrying a whistle"], 1, "Moving on takes people further from where searchers will look."],
    [2, "In Australia, which agency receives signals from personal locator beacons?", ["The Bureau of Meteorology", "The Australian Taxation Office", "The Australian Maritime Safety Authority", "Local councils"], 2, "AMSA receives beacon signals and coordinates with police and rescue agencies."],
    [3, "What does the STOP approach stand for?", ["Sprint, Travel, Observe, Pause", "Stop, Think, Observe, Plan", "Signal, Track, Orient, Proceed", "Stay, Talk, Open, Pray"], 1, "Stop, Think, Observe, Plan helps you avoid panicked decisions."],
    [4, "According to the rough rule of threes, which priority comes before water?", ["Shelter from harsh conditions", "Food", "Finding a road", "Lighting a large fire"], 0, "Protection from harsh weather is usually the most urgent need."],
    [5, "Which is widely recognised as a distress signal?", ["One long shout", "Two waves of the arm", "Three of anything, such as three whistle blasts", "Four rocks in a square"], 2, "Three signals in a row is a widely recognised call for help."]
  ]);
